# Prospective native percentile algorithm

This separate ignored stage retains the rejected two-module study and implements a new native selector plus a narrow shared-percentile dispatch change. It imports only its own newly written helper. Released Grafana/Lodash code runs in acceptance tools only. Production and every older frozen study remain unchanged. No timing, memory measurement, profiling, integration, universal correctness or superiority claim is made.

`ALGORITHM-PLAN.md` fixed the strategy and limits before candidate execution. Finite numeric samples use a private single-pass copy and median-of-nine sampled pivot for larger partitions. Once cumulative partition scans exceed twice the original length, pivoting uses deterministic groups of five. The conservative charge cap is `64*n+128`; refusal preserves the private array's complete permutation. Work charges count partition visits and insertion comparisons, with up to three reserved comparisons for a small sampled pivot. They are logical correctness diagnostics, not elapsed CPU, allocation or machine-resource measurements. Default-budget selection completed every new finite acceptance case; the charged cap does not establish a universal linear-time or machine-time guarantee.

Undefined, holes in modes that preserve them, nonfinite values, signed zero, coercible samples and all rejected fast-path inputs retain the authentic strict-null transformations and stable numeric sort. With ignore mode, holes are skipped just as `filter` skips them. Null-as-zero maps strict null to positive zero. The fallback path preserves the released comparator's unusual behavior for NaN/coercible values rather than imposing a different ordering. Arbitrary accessors/proxies/classes/boxed samples and mutable registries remain outside the verified ordinary-field/primitive-array contract.

Multi-percentile requests still share one complete stable sort. Direct assignment avoids a temporary result object and repeated special-reducer dispatch at each original queue position. Standard arithmetic, non-percentile special bodies, fixed registry and transport remain byte-identical. The entry differs from the frozen two-module control by precisely its import and this loop substitution; `candidate-contract.json` authenticates those edits and preserved section hashes. The earlier pre-acceptance contract is retained after manual-swap and midpoint refinements made before any acceptance run.

The first source-stable acceptance ran 2026-10-12 22:42:45–22:43:59 UTC, all five processes exiting normally:

- Replayed the established 2,910,751 reducer comparisons, including 48,240 cache cases, and 14,240 standard-helper checks. This is the same established generator/exhaustive corpus, not millions of newly independent inputs. The unchanged copied receipt fields describe the generator's historical independence from the earlier research study; they do not claim independence from previous optimization-stage replays.
- Replayed 3,888 held-out reducer and 864 direct registry checks, then 9,600 long standard/coercion checks, 1,920 helper cases and 24 caller/cache mutation cases.
- Replayed the exact 42-input, 9,984-comparison selector campaign against unchanged baseline, frozen two-module control and new candidate. Its graph fixture remains exact, including sparse holes, undefined, special numbers and aliases. The 9,984 total already includes registry, multi-selection, cache and exception subcounts.
- New seed `0xd3094e71` supplies 66 finite shapes up to 25,000 samples, 462 independently executed original percentile expectations and 462 exact default-budget selections. Another 3,444 deliberately reduced-budget calls included 2,662 refusals; every call retained the entire permutation and every fallback rank matched the authentic expected value. The corpus executed 525 deterministic pivot calls. The budget-refusal API is an internal finite-array control that mutates its supplied private array; it is not a newly supported application API for arbitrary caller values.
- The unchanged seven fixed measured input shapes passed 14 prior/new candidate comparisons against the original, including all 99 percentiles and all 124 calculations. The organ-pipe 25,000-sample p95 control completed selection with two deterministic pivots and no charged-cap fallback. This is branch correctness evidence only.

No candidate acceptance attempt failed. Both entry/helper and all test/control/actual original sources are copied before execution and authenticated after it. `preservation.json` binds the exact rejected measured review and original controls. The post-acceptance budget fixture is regenerated from the exact executed generator fragment and authenticated against all 462 executed input hashes; its preservation receipt identifies this later artifact creation honestly.

Two freeze-tool attempts failed while adapting the older manifest: its rows are named `entries` and are an array carrying path fields, while the reader first expected `inventory` and then a keyed map. Each initial failure is in the tool transcript; identical captured replays preserve both exact failed sources and outputs before the isolated metadata-reader corrections. Candidate/runtime/test sources and acceptance results were unaffected. This artifact-tool failure is retained rather than counted as a candidate compatibility failure.

The genuine authority remains `@grafana/data@13.2.3`, registry integrity, original release archive SHA-256 `954a03a68fa122ebe74c0c318dc801e434c17294c5374ef593cf491f73c81c41`, actual original CJS reducers and resolved Lodash 4.17.23 archive SHA-256 `9aba685f4b44d7a8e778cdf7085e9512143dabd78812df40e258375e1e8dceb4`. `authenticate-original.mjs` verifies retained official source/docs, actual archive members and actual dependency resolution. Six executed original runtime files are preserved with the acceptance snapshot. Archives remain local in the official artifact area; this candidate never loads them at runtime.

The complete default-cap `singlePercentile` refusal branch did not occur in the finite acceptance corpus. Reduced-cap direct-helper refusal/permutation checks and source review support that boundary; they are not presented as an observed default-cap refusal. Cache completeness, merge ordering, return-versus-cache shallow aliases, input mutation, unknown/duplicate selections, strict/truthy null flags, empty/missing shortcut behavior and error classes remain exercised by the original campaigns. Engine-specific stack/message text, every possible input and full Grafana product behavior remain unverified.

No future measurement input, runner or threshold is selected here. Any future timing/memory comparison needs a new coordinated root quiet interval and source bindings for both candidate modules. The preceding 27.91056% fixed fallback regression and remaining timing/memory regressions remain historical facts about the rejected control. This candidate is not accepted for production and is not assumed faster. A future separate helper import would also require root review of the static host's module allowance.
